# .NET backend standards

- Confirm target framework and hosting model before using APIs. Do not assume modern .NET APIs exist in .NET Framework.
- Follow the existing architecture. For new services, prefer clear boundaries (API/Presentation, Application, Domain, Infrastructure) without creating layers that add no value.
- Keep controllers/endpoints thin. Put use-case orchestration in application handlers/services; keep domain invariants in domain logic.
- Use asynchronous I/O end-to-end for network/database operations; accept and pass `CancellationToken` where supported. Do not wrap synchronous I/O in `Task.Run`.
- Use DI with appropriate lifetimes. Never capture scoped services in singletons; avoid service locator patterns.
- Use typed options and startup validation for configuration. Fail fast on missing mandatory settings without printing secrets.
- API contracts: explicit request/response DTOs, stable status codes, validation, pagination, correlation IDs, consistent problem details, and versioning strategy where needed. Never bind persistence entities directly from untrusted request bodies.
- Use `IHttpClientFactory` in modern .NET; for .NET Framework follow compatible lifetime/handler guidance and avoid creating/discarding clients per request. Set explicit timeouts and safe retry policy.
- Retries only for transient failures and idempotent operations; bound attempts and total time, add jitter, honor `Retry-After`, and avoid retry storms. Never blindly retry non-idempotent writes.
- Use structured logging and metrics. Do not log secrets, full auth headers, or unrestricted request/response bodies.
- Avoid static mutable state for request/user/tenant data. Propagate identity and tenant context explicitly and safely.
- Keep business rules independent of HTTP, database, and UI frameworks where practical. Do not expose EF entities as public API contracts.
- Add unit tests for business rules and integration tests for persistence/auth/API boundaries. Do not mock every dependency if a real database integration test is feasible.
- Performance: measure before optimizing; use bounded concurrency, streaming for large payloads, pagination, indexes based on query plans, and avoid N+1 queries.
